        "updated_assessment": "Two concrete economic-regulatory developments this week add texture to the prior mixed-signal assessment. Estonia is taking a deregulatory EU stance on the pay transparency directive \u2014 seeking a two-year delay and redrafting \u2014 while simultaneously cancelling planned domestic excise duty increases to cushion energy price impacts from the Iran conflict. Both actions reflect the same underlying pressure: defence-driven fiscal austerity constrains policy space, and the government is choosing to offload regulatory burden rather than add to it. The pay transparency episode also reveals a coalition credibility problem: Tsahkna personally approved the directive three years ago and has now reversed, drawing a public contradiction from his own party (Eesti 200). The competing interpretation: Estonia is acting in line with broader EU competitiveness discourse (the Commission's own Draghi report), and Sweden shares its position \u2014 this is not a rightward lurch but a calibrated deregulatory posture consistent with Estonia's structural economic preferences (per \u00a76, flat tax, retained earnings at 0%).",
