        "updated_assessment": "The domestic regime situation has deepened across all three active stress vectors. First, the Karis-Tsahkna presidential dispute enters its second week and has now been elevated to a systemic debate: Tsahkna and Reinsalu traded 'Orb\u00e1nism' accusations in a public radio debate, while Postimees analysis confirms the row has not changed the presidential race dynamics (Karis still popular but coalition prefers another candidate). Second, April polling confirms the coalition's structural precarity: Isamaa at 24%, Centre at 23%, coalition parties combined at 16%, with EKRE falling to 11% \u2014 but EKRE's decline is accompanied by radicalization signals, not moderation. Third, the Helme Hungary reaction and the Viljandi councillor's 'take up arms' post reveal an EKRE in internal turbulence: losing support in polls while its leadership escalates rhetoric and local officials test extremist language. Fourth, the EU pay transparency coalition split \u2014 Eesti 200 publicly contradicting Tsahkna \u2014 adds a new visible fissure to a coalition already operating on a razor-thin majority. The structural interpretation: Estonia's governing coalition is becoming visibly incoherent on EU policy, while opposition forces simultaneously consolidate (Isamaa) and radicalize at margins (EKRE). The March 2027 election horizon is driving escalating positioning behaviour across all actors. Competing interpretation: The Isamaa surge to 24% reflects mainstream conservative absorption of Reform's former base \u2014 this is system stabilisation, not destabilisation. EKRE at 11% and falling means the most destabilising political force is weakening electorally. The coalition's problems are electoral liabilities, not governance failures \u2014 Estonia's institutions are functioning normally under political stress.",
